ORIGINAL VERDICT (2026-09-22): UNMEASURED -- BENCH LIMIT (no wire-rate source, no second generator).

The case method: "Transmit packet from IXIA1 at wirerate. Then transmit pause
packet from IXIA2. Confirm that IXIA2 receives the packet at 1/2 rate. Also,
DUT sends Pause packet to IXIA1 in order to suppress original rate."

Three things it needs that this bench does not have:

1. **Wire rate.** 802.3x flow control only engages when a port is genuinely
   congested. This harness injects with scapy at ~26 Mbps -- 2.6% of a gigabit
   port -- so the DUT never reaches the pressure that triggers a pause frame.
2. **Two independent generators.** IXIA1 and IXIA2 are separate sources, one
   offering load and one injecting pause frames. There is one usable ingress
   port on this bench (each IE520 member has a single copper port).
3. **A rate measurement**, to show the receiver dropping to half rate. That is a
   sustained-throughput measurement, not a packet count.

Pause frames themselves could be crafted with scapy, and `polarity mdi` could be
configured, but neither would demonstrate the case's assertion: that flow
control CHANGES THE RATE under congestion. Configuring the feature and calling
it tested would be the wrong answer.

TO UNBLOCK: a line-rate traffic generator with two ports -- the same
requirement as QoS 13549/13553 and the guest-VLAN rate cases.
